\section*{Purpose of the Project}
%Figure out how to format this nicely
The aim of this project is to implement Black--Scholes option pricing 
theory in Python. This work is being done for my own personal 
development and enjoyment, but also to strengthen my understanding of 
how mathematical models can be translated into working financial code.

These notes act as a complementary guide to the project. They outline 
the main theory behind the Black--Scholes model, derive the pricing 
formula for European call and put options, and explain how the 
mathematical formula is implemented computationally. In addition, there 
may be further sections introducing other type contingent claims.

It should be noted that these notes do not cover all of the probability and measure theory required for a fully rigorous treatment of the Black--Scholes model. In particular, topics such as Brownian motion, stochastic integration, martingales, filtrations and equivalent martingale measures are only discussed at the level needed for this project. A selection of textbooks are included at the end which act as references but the main content is adapted from the course in probability, measure and finance from the university of Edinburgh taught by Leonardo Tolomeo.

\section*{The Black--Scholes Model}
% 
To begin, we will work on a filtered probability space, which is given as:
\[
    (\Omega,\mathcal{F},(\mathcal{F}_t)_{t\geq 0},\Prob),
\]
supporting a Brownian motion \(W=(W_t)_{t\geq 0}\) and the filtration \((\mathcal{F}_t)_{t\geq 0}\) represents the information available to the market up to time \(t\).

In the Black--Scholes model, the market consists of two assets:

\begin{itemize}
    \item a risky asset, usually interpreted as a stock;
    \item a riskless asset, usually interpreted as a bank account or bond.
\end{itemize}

The stock price \(S_t\) follows the stochastic differential equation (SDE):
\[
    \dd S_t = \mu S_t \dd t + \sigma S_t \dd W_t,
    \qquad S_0>0,
    \tag{1}
\]
where \(\mu\) is the drift (expected rate of return) and \(\sigma>0\) is the volatility.

The riskless asset \(B_t\) satisfies
\[
    \dd B_t = rB_t \dd t,
    \qquad B_0=1,
    \tag{2}
\]
where \(r\) is the continuously compounded risk-free interest rate.

In the classical Black--Scholes model, \(\mu\), \(r\) and \(\sigma\) are assumed to be constant. Under these assumptions, the solutions are:
\[
    S_t = S_0 \exp\left(\left(\mu-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2\right)t+\sigma W_t\right),
    \tag{1A}
\]
and
\[
    B_t = e^{rt}.
    \tag{2A}
\]

The stock price is therefore lognormally distributed. This is one of 
the key reasons why the Black--Scholes formula can be written in closed form.

