\begin{definition}[num\'eraire]

A num\'eraire is a strictly positive price process used as a unit of account. If \(X_t\) is an asset price and \(N_t>0\) is a num\'eraire, then the discounted or relative price of \(X_t\) in units of \(N_t\) is:
\[
    \frac{X_t}{N_t}.
\]

\end{definition}

Using the bank account \(B_t=e^{rt}\) as num\'eraire, the discounted stock price, $\widetilde{S}_t$, is:

\[
    \widetilde{S}_t = \frac{S_t}{B_t}=e^{-rt}S_t.
\]


\section*{Discounted Prices and No-Arbitrage Intuition}

It is convenient to work with discounted prices because it expresses 
all values in units of the riskless asset. That is, instead of 
measuring wealth in pounds, we measure wealth relative to the bank
account \(B_t\). This removes the deterministic growth due to the risk-free interest rate.


We now introduce one of the most important formulas in stochastic calculus, It\"o's formula, for which we frsit define an It\"o process:

\begin{definition}[It\^o process]
An It\^o process is a stochastic process \(X=(X_t)_{t\geq 0}\) that can be written in the following form,
\[
    X_t
    =
    X_0
    +
    \int_0^t a_s \dd s
    +
    \int_0^t b_s \dd W_s,
\]
where \(W_t\) is a Brownian motion and \(a_t\), \(b_t\) are adapted processes satisfying suitable integrability conditions, i.e for every $T>0  $, we have that  $ \int_0^T |a_s|^2\dd s < \infty$ and $\int_0^T |b_s|^2\dd s < \infty $ a.s .

As well as this, we equivalently have in differential notation the following: 
\[
    \dd X_t = a_t\dd t + b_t\dd W_t.
\]
The term \(a_t\) is called the drift coefficient, and \(b_t\) is called the diffusion coefficient.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[It\"o's formula]

Let \(X_t\) be an It\^o process satisfying
\[
    \dd X_t = a_t\dd t + b_t\dd W_t,
\]
where \(a_t\) and \(b_t\) are adapted processes. If \(f\in C^2(\R)\), then \(f(X_t)\) is also an
It\^o process and satisfies
\[
    \dd f(X_t)
    =
    f'(X_t)\dd X_t
    +
    \frac{1}{2}f''(X_t)(\dd X_t)^2.
\]
Equivalently,
\[
    \dd f(X_t)
    =
    \left(
        a_t f'(X_t)
        +
        \frac{1}{2}b_t^2 f''(X_t)
    \right)\dd t
    +
    b_t f'(X_t)\dd W_t.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The additional second-order term
\[
    \frac{1}{2}b_t^2f''(X_t)\dd t
\]
is the key difference between It\^o calculus and ordinary calculus. It appears because
Brownian motion has quadratic variation \((\dd W_t)^2=\dd t\). 
\end{remark}


