Now we have It\"o's formula, we can apply it to the discounted stock price, which gives us:
\[
    \dd \widetilde{S}_t
    =
    \dd(e^{-rt}S_t).
\]

Since
\[
    \dd(e^{-rt})=-re^{-rt}\dd t,
\]
we obtain
\[
    \dd \widetilde{S}_t
    =
    e^{-rt}\dd S_t - r e^{-rt}S_t\dd t.
\]

Substituting in the Black--Scholes stock dynamics
\[
    \dd S_t = \mu S_t\dd t+\sigma S_t\dd W_t,
\]
gives
\[
    \dd \widetilde{S}_t
    =
    e^{-rt}(\mu S_t\dd t+\sigma S_t\dd W_t)
    -re^{-rt}S_t\dd t.
\]

Therefore,
\[
    \dd \widetilde{S}_t
    =
    (\mu-r)\widetilde{S}_t\dd t
    +\sigma \widetilde{S}_t\dd W_t.
\]

This shows that the discounted stock price still has a drift term under the original probability measure \(\Prob\), unless \(\mu=r\). In general, the real-world expected return \(\mu\) is not equal to the risk-free rate \(r\).

The key idea behind risk-neutral pricing is that we can move to a new probability measure \(\Q\), called the risk-neutral measure, under which the discounted stock price has no drift. Under this measure,
\[
    \dd \widetilde{S}_t
    =
    \sigma \widetilde{S}_t\dd W_t^{\Q}.
\]

Thus, under \(\Q\), the discounted stock price is a martingale. This means that its expected future discounted value is equal to its current value:
\[
    \widetilde{S}_t
    =
    \E^{\Q}\left[\widetilde{S}_T \mid \mathcal{F}_t\right].
\]

This martingale property is central to modern derivative pricing. It means that once prices are discounted by the riskless asset, there should be no predictable way to make a risk-free profit. This is closely related to the no-arbitrage principle.

\section*{Self-Financing Portfolios}

A trading strategy is a choice of how much wealth to hold in the stock and how much to hold in the riskless asset. Suppose a portfolio holds \(\Delta_t\) units of the stock and \(\beta_t\) units of the bank account. The value of the portfolio is
\[
    V_t = \Delta_t S_t + \beta_t B_t.
\]

The portfolio is called self-financing if changes in its value come only from changes in the prices of the assets already held. That is, no money is added to or withdrawn from the portfolio after it is set up.

Mathematically, a self-financing portfolio satisfies
\[
    \dd V_t = \Delta_t \dd S_t + \beta_t \dd B_t.
\]

This idea is important because the Black--Scholes argument is based on replicating a derivative payoff using a self-financing trading strategy. If a portfolio can be constructed so that
\[
    V_T = h(S_T),
\]
then, by no-arbitrage, the price of the derivative at time \(0\) must be equal to the initial cost \(V_0\) of the replicating portfolio.

If this were not true, then an arbitrage opportunity would exist. For example, if the derivative were cheaper than the replicating portfolio, one could buy the derivative and sell the portfolio. If it were more expensive, one could sell the derivative and buy the portfolio. In either case, this would create a risk-free profit, which is ruled out by the no-arbitrage assumption.


\section*{Risk-Neutral Pricing}

The Black--Scholes model prices derivatives by using a risk-neutral probability measure, usually denoted by \(\Q\). Under this measure, the discounted stock price is a martingale. Informally, this means that after discounting by the risk-free rate, the stock earns no additional expected return under \(\Q\).

Under the risk-neutral measure, the stock price dynamics become
\[
    \dd S_t = rS_t \dd t + \sigma S_t \dd W_t^{\Q},
\]
where \(W_t^{\Q}\) is a Brownian motion under \(\Q\). The solution is
\[
    S_T = S_0 \exp\left(\left(r-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2\right)T+\sigma W_T^{\Q}\right).
\]


Therefore, for a European option with payoff \(h(S_T)\), the no-arbitrage price at time \(0\) is
\[
    V_0 = e^{-rT}\E^{\Q}\left[h(S_T)\right].
\]

This formula is the theoretical foundation of the Black--Scholes pricing formula.


\section*{European Type Contingent Claims}

A contingent claim is a financial contract whose value depends on the value of some underlying asset. In this project, the underlying asset is the stock price \(S_t\).

A European option is a contract that can only be exercised at maturity \(T\). This is different from an American option, which can be exercised at any time \(t\in[0,T]\).

A European-type contingent claim has payoff
\[
    h = g(S_T),
\]
where \(g\) is a non-negative Borel measurable function.

For a European call option with strike price \(K\), the payoff is
\[
    C_T = \max(S_T-K,0) = (S_T-K)^+.
\]

For a European put option with strike price \(K\), the payoff is
\[
    P_T = \max(K-S_T,0) = (K-S_T)^+.
\]

The call option gives the holder the right, but not the obligation, to buy the stock at price \(K\) at maturity. The put option gives the holder the right, but not the obligation, to sell the stock at price \(K\) at maturity.

