\section{The Black--Scholes Pricing Formula}
\label{sec:black-scholes-formula}

The Black--Scholes formula gives closed-form prices for European call and put options.
It is obtained by applying the risk-neutral valuation formula to the payoff of a European option.

Under the risk-neutral measure \(\Q\), the stock price satisfies
\[
    \dd S_t = rS_t\dd t+\sigma S_t\dd W_t^{\Q}.
\]
Therefore,
\[
    S_T
    =
    S_0
    \exp\left(
        \left(r-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2\right)T
        +
        \sigma W_T^{\Q}
    \right).
\]
Since \(W_T^{\Q}\sim N(0,T)\), we can write
\[
    W_T^{\Q}=\sqrt{T}Z,
    \qquad
    Z\sim N(0,1).
\]
Hence
\[
    S_T
    =
    S_0
    \exp\left(
        \left(r-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2\right)T
        +
        \sigma\sqrt{T}Z
    \right).
\]
This shows that \(S_T\) is lognormally distributed under the risk-neutral measure.

\begin{theorem}[Black--Scholes formula]
\label{thm:black-scholes-formula}
In the Black--Scholes model, the time-zero price of a European call option with strike
\(K\) and maturity \(T\) is
\[
    C = S_0N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2),
\]
and the time-zero price of a European put option with the same strike and maturity is
\[
    P = Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2)-S_0N(-d_1),
\]
where
\[
    d_1 =
    \frac{\ln(S_0/K)+\left(r+\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2\right)T}
    {\sigma\sqrt{T}},
    \qquad
    d_2 = d_1-\sigma\sqrt{T}.
\]
\end{theorem}

Here \(N(x)\) denotes the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal
distribution:
\[
    N(x)
    =
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
    \int_{-\infty}^{x} e^{-z^2/2}\dd z.
\]

\begin{proof}[Sketch of the call option derivation]
The payoff of a European call option is
\[
    (S_T-K)^+.
\]
By risk-neutral valuation, its time-zero price is
\[
    C
    =
    e^{-rT}\E^{\Q}\left[(S_T-K)^+\right].
\]
This can be written as
\[
    C
    =
    e^{-rT}\E^{\Q}\left[(S_T-K)\mathbf{1}_{\{S_T>K\}}\right].
\]
Expanding the expectation gives
\[
    C
    =
    e^{-rT}
    \left(
        \E^{\Q}\left[S_T\mathbf{1}_{\{S_T>K\}}\right]
        -
        K\Q(S_T>K)
    \right).
\]

Using the lognormal distribution of \(S_T\), one obtains
\[
    \Q(S_T>K)=N(d_2),
\]
and
\[
    e^{-rT}\E^{\Q}\left[S_T\mathbf{1}_{\{S_T>K\}}\right]
    =
    S_0N(d_1).
\]
Therefore,
\[
    C
    =
    S_0N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2).
\]
The put option formula follows either by a similar calculation or from put--call parity.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The Black--Scholes formula does not depend on the real-world expected return \(\mu\).
This is because the option is priced under the risk-neutral measure \(\Q\), where the stock
has drift \(r\), not \(\mu\). The inputs to the formula are therefore
\[
    S_0,\quad K,\quad T,\quad r,\quad \sigma.
\]
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Interpretation of \(d_1\) and \(d_2\)]
The quantities \(d_1\) and \(d_2\) measure the moneyness of the option after adjusting for
interest rates, time to maturity and volatility.

The term \(N(d_2)\) can be interpreted as the risk-neutral probability that the call option
finishes in the money:
\[
    N(d_2)=\Q(S_T>K).
\]
The term \(N(d_1)\) is related to the sensitivity of the call price with respect to the current
stock price. In fact, for a European call option, \(N(d_1)\) is the option's Delta:
\[
    \Delta_{\text{call}} = \frac{\partial C}{\partial S_0}=N(d_1).
\]
\end{remark}



\section*{Put--Call Parity}

Put--call parity is an important relationship between European call and put prices with the same strike price \(K\) and maturity \(T\). It is given by
\[
    C-P = S_0-Ke^{-rT}.
\]
This identity can be useful for checking the implementation. That is, if the Python program returns call and put prices that do not approximately satisfy put--call parity, then there is likely an error in the calculation.

\section*{Implementation Details}

The Python program takes the following inputs:
\[
    S_0,\quad K,\quad T,\quad r,\quad \sigma.
\]

These represent:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
\toprule
Symbol & Meaning \\
\midrule
\(S_0\) & Initial stock price \\
\(K\) & Strike price \\
\(T\) & Time to maturity \\
\(r\) & Risk-free interest rate \\
\(\sigma\) & Volatility of the stock \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The program then follows these steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Compute \(d_1\):
    \[
        d_1 =
        \frac{\ln(S_0/K)+\left(r+\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2\right)T}
        {\sigma\sqrt{T}}.
    \]

    \item Compute \(d_2\):
    \[
        d_2=d_1-\sigma\sqrt{T}.
    \]

    \item Evaluate the standard normal cumulative distribution function at \(d_1\), \(d_2\), \(-d_1\) and \(-d_2\).

    \item Return the call price:
    \[
        C = S_0N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2).
    \]

    \item Return the put price:
    \[
        P = Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2)-S_0N(-d_1).
    \]
\end{enumerate}

In Python, the normal cumulative distribution function can be computed using \texttt{scipy.stats.norm.cdf}. The exponential and logarithmic terms can be computed using either the standard \texttt{math} library or \texttt{numpy}.

\section*{Numerical Example}

We consider the following usual example, which is taken from :
\[
    S_0=100,\qquad K=100,\qquad T=1,\qquad r=0.05,\qquad \sigma=0.2.
\]

Then
\[
    d_1 =
    \frac{\ln(100/100)+\left(0.05+\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^2\right)(1)}
    {0.2\sqrt{1}}
    =0.35,
\]
and
\[
    d_2 = 0.35-0.2=0.15.
\]

The Black--Scholes call price is approximately
\[
    C \approx 10.4506,
\]
and the put price is approximately
\[
    P \approx 5.5735.
\]

We can also verify put--call parity:
\[
    C-P \approx 10.4506-5.5735 = 4.8771,
\]
while
\[
    S_0-Ke^{-rT}
    =
    100-100e^{-0.05}
    \approx 4.8771.
\]

Therefore, the numerical output is consistent with put--call parity.

\section*{Ideas for Further Work}

This project implements the Black--Scholes pricing formula for European call and put options. The mathematical theory begins with modelling the stock price as a geometric Brownian motion and using risk-neutral pricing to compute the discounted expected payoff of an option.

Possible extensions to this project include adding dividend yields, pricing American Options, Exotic Options, calculating Greeks, plotting option prices as functions of input parameters, and comparing Black--Scholes prices with Monte Carlo estimates.

Another idea was to implement an nice-looking interface for the option calculations.



